WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.373
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book three.

2
00:00:05.673 --> 00:00:09.327
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.627 --> 00:00:16.993
Unit twenty-eight: Southeast Asian History. Today you will link connected historical points with formal additive connectors.

4
00:00:17.293 --> 00:00:19.875
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.833 --> 00:00:24.182
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.017 --> 00:00:26.366
Southeast Asia.

7
00:00:27.350 --> 00:00:31.509
Southeast Asia means the region between India, China and Australia.

8
00:00:32.117 --> 00:00:34.917
Southeast Asia includes eleven countries.

9
00:00:35.517 --> 00:00:38.227
Your turn. Say: Southeast Asia.

10
00:00:41.067 --> 00:00:42.544
Southeast Asia.

11
00:00:43.283 --> 00:00:44.157
Kingdom.

12
00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:48.032
Kingdom means a country ruled by a king or queen.

13
00:00:48.617 --> 00:00:51.731
The Khmer kingdom dominated the region for centuries.

14
00:00:52.317 --> 00:00:54.632
Your turn. Say: Kingdom.

15
00:00:57.467 --> 00:00:58.305
Kingdom.

16
00:00:59.050 --> 00:00:59.761
Trade.

17
00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:03.562
Trade means the exchange of goods and services.

18
00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:06.033
Trade linked the region's ports.

19
00:01:06.650 --> 00:01:09.163
Your turn. Say: Trade.

20
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:12.804
Trade.

21
00:01:13.550 --> 00:01:14.458
Religion.

22
00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:18.169
Religion means a system of faith and worship.

23
00:01:18.783 --> 00:01:22.199
Theravada Buddhism spread across mainland Southeast Asia.

24
00:01:22.783 --> 00:01:25.111
Your turn. Say: Religion.

25
00:01:27.967 --> 00:01:29.026
Religion.

26
00:01:29.783 --> 00:01:30.657
Temple.

27
00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:34.149
Temple means a building for religious worship.

28
00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:37.388
Borobudur and Angkor Wat are famous temples.

29
00:01:37.983 --> 00:01:40.520
Your turn. Say: Temple.

30
00:01:43.367 --> 00:01:44.089
Temple.

31
00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:45.677
Culture.

32
00:01:46.683 --> 00:01:50.134
Culture means the customs, arts and beliefs of a group.

33
00:01:50.750 --> 00:01:53.318
Rice farming shaped the region's culture.

34
00:01:53.917 --> 00:01:56.302
Your turn. Say: Culture.

35
00:01:59.167 --> 00:02:00.017
Culture.

36
00:02:00.783 --> 00:02:01.610
Region.

37
00:02:02.617 --> 00:02:05.557
Region means a large area with shared features.

38
00:02:06.150 --> 00:02:08.254
The region shares a monsoon climate.

39
00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:11.305
Your turn. Say: Region.

40
00:02:14.167 --> 00:02:15.063
Region.

41
00:02:15.817 --> 00:02:16.771
Heritage.

42
00:02:17.783 --> 00:02:21.292
Heritage means traditions and buildings passed down from the past.

43
00:02:21.883 --> 00:02:24.220
ASEAN countries share a rich heritage.

44
00:02:24.817 --> 00:02:27.457
Your turn. Say: Heritage.

45
00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:31.220
Heritage.

46
00:02:32.033 --> 00:02:35.812
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:36.650 --> 00:02:42.272
Visal, what connects the histories of Cambodia, Thailand, Laos, Myanmar and Vietnam?

48
00:02:43.383 --> 00:02:54.769
Several things. Indian influence shaped early kingdoms' religion and writing. Moreover, trade linked the region's kingdoms through rivers and ports.

49
00:02:55.883 --> 00:02:57.186
Anything else?

50
00:02:58.283 --> 00:03:08.294
Furthermore, Theravada Buddhism became the main religion in most of the mainland, so temples, festivals and monastic life are remarkably similar.

51
00:03:09.383 --> 00:03:10.918
And in the modern period?

52
00:03:12.017 --> 00:03:23.667
Most were colonised, except Thailand. In addition, all experienced nationalist movements, and today ASEAN builds on this shared heritage.

53
00:03:24.833 --> 00:03:26.659
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:27.517 --> 00:03:31.014
Grammar focus: Formal linking (Moreover and Furthermore).

55
00:03:31.314 --> 00:03:32.536
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:33.250 --> 00:03:38.317
Indian influence shaped early kingdoms. Moreover, trade linked the region's kingdoms.

57
00:03:39.217 --> 00:03:44.168
Rivers connected inland states. Furthermore, Buddhism created shared traditions.

58
00:03:45.083 --> 00:03:49.489
Most states were colonised. In addition, all faced nationalist movements.

59
00:03:50.433 --> 00:04:00.310
'Moreover', 'furthermore' and 'in addition' add a further, often stronger, point. They begin a new sentence (or follow a semicolon) and are followed by a comma. Avoid them in casual speech.

60
00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:05.165
Be careful! Don't say: Moreover trade linked the kingdoms.

61
00:04:07.100 --> 00:04:09.796
Say: Moreover, trade linked the kingdoms.

62
00:04:10.096 --> 00:04:11.910
Put a comma after 'Moreover'.

63
00:04:12.700 --> 00:04:15.178
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:15.967 --> 00:04:17.734
A Region of Connections.

65
00:04:18.034 --> 00:04:23.865
Southeast Asia has often been described as a crossroads between the civilizations of India and China.

66
00:04:24.867 --> 00:04:32.811
Early states such as Funan and Champa adopted Indian scripts, religions and political ideas, adapting them to local traditions.

67
00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:38.992
Moreover, river systems such as the Mekong encouraged trade and contact between inland kingdoms.

68
00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:45.378
Furthermore, the spread of Theravada Buddhism created shared cultural practices across much of the mainland.

69
00:04:46.367 --> 00:04:52.674
In addition, the experience of colonialism and independence continues to shape regional cooperation today.

70
00:04:53.767 --> 00:04:57.734
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:58.583 --> 00:05:02.766
Rice is central to the economy. blank, it shapes local festivals.

72
00:05:03.066 --> 00:05:06.580
A, Moreover. B, However. C, Although.

73
00:05:10.133 --> 00:05:12.098
The answer is A: Moreover.

74
00:05:13.250 --> 00:05:15.261
Choose the correct punctuation.

75
00:05:15.561 --> 00:05:22.291
A, Furthermore trade grew quickly.. B, Furthermore, trade grew quickly.. C, Furthermore; trade, grew quickly..

76
00:05:25.833 --> 00:05:28.553
The answer is B: Furthermore, trade grew quickly..

77
00:05:29.717 --> 00:05:33.156
Traditions and buildings passed down from the past are blank.

78
00:05:33.456 --> 00:05:36.633
A, heritage. B, region. C, kingdom.

79
00:05:40.167 --> 00:05:42.491
The answer is A: heritage.

80
00:05:43.650 --> 00:05:46.613
Which country in Southeast Asia was never colonised?

81
00:05:46.913 --> 00:05:50.601
A, Cambodia. B, Vietnam. C, Thailand.

82
00:05:54.167 --> 00:05:56.166
The answer is C: Thailand.

83
00:05:57.317 --> 00:06:00.907
According to the reading, what encouraged contact between inland kingdoms?

84
00:06:01.207 --> 00:06:07.171
A, river systems such as the Mekong. B, colonialism. C, the Indian Ocean only.

85
00:06:10.733 --> 00:06:14.010
The answer is A: river systems such as the Mekong.

86
00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:18.363
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:19.217 --> 00:06:22.377
What connects the histories of Southeast Asian countries?

88
00:06:39.183 --> 00:06:40.695
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:40.995 --> 00:07:22.215
Several factors connect Southeast Asian histories. First, many early kingdoms were influenced by Indian religions, writing systems and political ideas. Moreover, trade along rivers and coasts linked communities across the region for centuries. Furthermore, most countries experienced colonial rule and later struggled for independence in similar ways. Each country, of course, also has its own unique story. Nevertheless, I believe these shared experiences provide a strong basis for regional cooperation.

90
00:07:22.515 --> 00:07:24.224
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:25.117 --> 00:07:30.195
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and formal linking (moreover and furthermore).

92
00:07:30.495 --> 00:07:35.644
Today's value is global unity. When the world works together, we all go further.

93
00:07:35.944 --> 00:07:39.897
See you in Unit twenty-nine. Goodbye!

94
00:07:40.197 --> 00:07:43.267
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

